---

<BaseLayout
  title="Óbidos, Nazaré and Alcobaça in one day: the Silver Coast loop from Pombal"
  description="Alcobaça, Nazaré and Óbidos in a single day from Abiul (Pombal): 244 km, about 3 hours of driving, real drive times, where to park and what to see in each town."
  canonical="/blog/obidos-nazare-alcobaca-day-trip-pombal/"
  locale="en"
  schema={schema}
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <div class="mb-8">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-muted font-body mb-4">
        <span class="bg-stone-100 text-wood-700 px-3 py-1">Day Trips</span>
        <span>October 2026</span>
        <span>&middot; 7 min read</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="leading-tight mb-4">Óbidos, Nazaré and Alcobaça in one day: the Silver Coast loop from Pombal</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-muted font-body">
        Yes, it fits in a day &mdash; and comfortably. From Abiul the full loop is <strong>244 km with about
        3 hours of driving</strong>: <strong>83 km (65 min)</strong> to Alcobaça, <strong>14 km (15 min)</strong> on to
        Nazaré, <strong>40 km (32 min)</strong> to Óbidos, then <strong>107 km (75 min)</strong> home. Leave at 9am and
        you are back at the pool by 7pm, with three of the most distinctive places in Central Portugal behind you.
      </p>
    </div>

    <OptimizedImage src="/images/airbnb/467c6107-9824-44ec-98f5-fcc2d62cb186.jpeg" alt="Casa dos Duques, the whitewashed holiday home with a private pool and lawn in Abiul, Pombal" class="w-full my-6" loading="lazy" width={1200} height={800} sizes="(max-width: 896px) 100vw, 896px" />

    <div class="text-muted leading-relaxed space-y-4 font-body">
      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Why these three, in this order</h2>
      <p>
        The three towns sit in a neat line on the <strong>Costa de Prata</strong>, and the order matters. Alcobaça opens
        early and is the quietest of the three in the morning. Nazaré is 15 minutes further west and is best at
        midday, when the light hits the cliff and the seafood restaurants along the beach are open. Óbidos is the
        perfect late afternoon &mdash; golden stone, walls to walk, and a last coffee before the motorway home.
        Driving the other way round puts you in Óbidos at 10am with the coach parties.
      </p>
      <p>
        The route uses the <strong>A1</strong> south towards Leiria, then the <strong>A8</strong> west to the coast, and
        returns the same way. Both are tolled motorways, so a toll transponder or a rental car with an electronic
        toll device saves time at the barriers. Our <a href="/blog/central-portugal-road-trip-by-car-ev/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">road-trip guide</a>
        covers the same corridor in more detail, including charging stops if you are driving an EV.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Stop 1 &mdash; Alcobaça: the first Gothic church in Portugal</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>Mosteiro de Santa Maria de Alcobaça</strong> was founded in 1153 by Afonso Henriques, the first king
        of Portugal, and handed to the Cistercian order. Construction of the church began in 1178 and was only
        completed in 1252 &mdash; which is why it reads as the earliest truly Gothic architecture in the country:
        soaring, bare, and built for acoustics and silence rather than decoration. UNESCO listed it in 1989.
      </p>
      <p>
        Do not miss the two tombs facing each other across the transept: <strong>King Pedro I and Inês de Castro</strong>.
        Inês was murdered on the orders of Pedro's father, and the story of the crowned corpse &mdash; the queen who was
        exhumed and enthroned after death &mdash; is the most told tale in Portugal. Also look for the medieval
        kitchen, lined with 18th-century azulejo tiles and built unusually tall so that the smoke from whole roasting
        animals had somewhere to go.
      </p>
      <p>
        Opening times and ticket prices change with the season and with religious holidays, so check the monastery's
        official page before you travel. Allow 60&ndash;90 minutes for the church, the cloisters and the kitchen, and
        leave time for a coffee in the monastery square.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Stop 2 &mdash; Nazaré: a canyon, a funicular and the biggest waves in the world</h2>
      <p>
        Nazaré's waves are not a marketing story. Just offshore runs the <strong>Nazaré Canyon</strong>, the largest
        submarine canyon in Europe: roughly <strong>230 km long and 5,000 m deep</strong>. It behaves like a funnel,
        accelerating and concentrating Atlantic swell so that waves break at heights found nowhere else on the
        planet. The world record for the largest wave ever surfed &mdash; 26.2 m, in 2020 &mdash; was set here.
      </p>
      <p>
        The town is split in two. <strong>Praia</strong>, at sea level, is the fishing quarter: restaurants, the beach,
        and the north end where the big-wave show happens. Above it, <strong>Sítio</strong> sits on the cliff, with the
        sanctuary of Nossa Senhora da Nazaré and the famous viewpoint over the beach. The two are linked by the
        <strong>Nazaré Funicular</strong>, a short, steep ride that saves you the drive up &mdash; and gives you the
        best photograph of the day.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>When to come for the waves:</strong> the giant swells belong to the Atlantic winter, roughly
        <strong>October to March</strong>. That is also when the cliff at Praia do Norte is busiest with photographers
        and the beach is closed to swimmers. In summer the same beach is a normal, family-friendly Atlantic beach
        &mdash; cold water, strong currents, lifeguards in season. If you want to combine waves with a swim, go in
        September: warm sea, and the first big swell of the season often arrives late in the month.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Stop 3 &mdash; Óbidos: a town inside its own walls</h2>
      <p>
        Óbidos is small enough to cross in fifteen minutes and interesting enough to keep you for three hours. The
        whole historic centre sits inside a ring of <strong>medieval walls</strong>, remodelled under King Dinis I, with
        the castle keep added in the 14th century. For centuries the town was given to Portuguese queens as a wedding
        gift, which is why it is still called the <em>Vila das Rainhas</em> &mdash; the town of the queens.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can walk the <strong>wall walk</strong> above the rooftops. It is free, unforgettable, and genuinely
        exposed: there is a handrail in places and a sheer drop in others, so it is not the walk for small children or
        anyone uneasy with heights. Inside, the main street runs from the Porta da Vila gate to the castle, lined with
        <strong>bookshops</strong> &mdash; Óbidos has built a second identity as Portugal's literary town, with shops in
        former churches and a literary festival each year. Order a <em>ginjinha</em>, the sour-cherry liqueur served in a
        chocolate cup, from one of the little counters near the gate.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Winter bonus:</strong> from <strong>27 November 2026 to 3 January 2027</strong> Óbidos holds its
        <em>Vila Natal</em> Christmas village &mdash; the medieval streets filled with lights, an ice rink, markets
        and family shows. It is the busiest Óbidos gets all year, and the reason to arrive early or stay
        late rather than pass through at midday.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">The winter version of this day</h2>
      <p>
        Between November and March the loop becomes a different trip, and honestly a better one for adults. Alcobaça's
        cloisters empty out, Nazaré puts on its big-wave theatre, and Óbidos glows. You will drive the same 244 km but
        stop more often, because parking is easy and nothing has a queue. Bring a windproof layer for the cliff at
        Nazaré &mdash; the wind there is not a breeze &mdash; and check the surf forecast the night before if the waves
        are the point of the trip.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Practical notes</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Parking</strong> &mdash; in Óbidos use the car parks outside the walls and walk in; the streets inside are pedestrian in practice and one-way in theory. In Nazaré, park at Sítio for the view or along the seafront at Praia.</li>
        <li><strong>Lunch</strong> &mdash; Nazaré is the place for it: grilled fish and seafood, a two-minute walk from the sand. Book on summer weekends and during Vila Natal.</li>
        <li><strong>Timing</strong> &mdash; Alcobaça 09:45&ndash;11:15, Nazaré 11:30&ndash;15:00 (lunch included), Óbidos 15:30&ndash;18:00, home by 19:15.</li>
        <li><strong>With children</strong> &mdash; swap the Óbidos wall walk for the castle exterior and the playground, and add the funicular at Nazaré; it is the highlight for most kids.</li>
        <li><strong>Tolls and fuel</strong> &mdash; both motorways are tolled; fill up before leaving Pombal, where fuel is cheaper than on the A8 service areas.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Where to base it</h2>
      <p>
        The reason this day works is the base. <a href="/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Casa dos Duques</a>
        sits in Abiul, 11 minutes from Pombal and a few minutes from the A1 &mdash; close enough to reach the coast and
        the monuments before the crowds, far enough that you come back to a garden, a pool and total silence instead of
        a hotel corridor. If you are planning several days of this, our
        <a href="/day-trips/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">day trips page</a> lists every
        destination within an hour with real drive times, and the
        <a href="/guide/central-portugal/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">Central Portugal guide</a>
        puts them in context. For a shorter coastal day, start with
        <a href="/beaches/" class="text-terracotta-500 hover:text-terracotta-600 underline">the beaches closest to the house</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        One warning: three towns in one day is a tasting menu, not a meal. If you have the time, split the loop in two
        and give Óbidos an evening of its own &mdash; the town is at its best after the day-trippers have gone and the
        walls turn gold.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-8 p-6 bg-stone-100">
      <p class="text-sm text-muted italic font-body">
        Sources: Óbidos Vila Natal official site (dates 27/11/2026&ndash;03/01/2027); Mosteiro de Alcobaça history and
        UNESCO listing (1989); Nazaré Canyon dimensions (largest submarine canyon in Europe, ~230 km long, ~5,000 m
        deep) and the 26.2 m wave record set in 2020. Distances and drive times calculated with OSRM from Abiul
        (39.8726, -8.5386); they are estimates, not guarantees. Opening times and prices change &mdash; always confirm
        with the venue before travelling.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <CTASection
    title="Three towns, one day, one quiet base"
    subtitle="Stay 11 minutes from the A1 in Abiul and reach Alcobaça, Nazaré and Óbidos before the crowds"
    primaryCta={{ label: 'Book your stay', href: '/booking/' }}
    variant="terracotta"
  />
</BaseLayout>
